{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "﻿The 1980s and 1990s were the \ngolden age of the American buffet. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For just six dollars at lunch or nine at \ndinner, Americans could feast on unlimited beef,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9040,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chicken, pasta, meatballs, potatoes, and \ndesserts at their local hometown buffet. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14000,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Down South, there was Golden Corral, who \nhad pivoted from a failing steakhouse  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17920,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into a flourishing, all-you-can-eat haven \nof steak, pot roast, and bread rolls. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22640,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And out West, Fresh Choice and Souplantation \ntraded the greasy meat and potatoes for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27600,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a healthy lineup of endless salads, \nsoups, muffins, and frozen yogurt. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32480,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Across these two decades, the industry \nboomed and the format spread far beyond  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36400,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these fast-growing chains. \nTheir rapid success caught the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39440,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attention of Las Vegas casinos, who \nbegan pouring money into buffets of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43200,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their own to draw tourists through the door.\nWhen the Rio’s Carnival World Buffet opened in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1993 with over 300 items, it kicked off \nan arms race to see who could offer the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52960,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most extravagant buffet on the Strip.\nAt the time, the idea of a dedicated,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57280,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "big-box buffet felt like a natural \nevolution of a growing industry trend. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Olive Garden had been serving unlimited \nbreadsticks, soup, and salad since opening. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66560,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pizza Hut had started lunch buffets to drive \ngreater traffic and sales earlier in the day. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Steakhouse chains like Ponderosa piled on endless \nsides and self-service salad bars to help sell  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76480,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the beef that customers paid full price for. \nWhenever the industry tried unlimited, it had  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81200,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worked. Yet it had never been the whole business.\nThe real leap in the 80s was extending the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85680,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "format from an add-on to the entire \nrestaurant, and the model proved so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simple that it made its way into the Midwest.\nFirst-generation Chinese immigrants opened buffets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94640,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as their doorway into the restaurant industry. \nThe business on paper stripped out everything  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99200,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hard about running a restaurant. \nThere were no servers to hire,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102400,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no menu to design, minimal English \nneeded, and no precision required. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106880,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The only work was to batch-cook pans of food, \nreheat frozen store-bought appetizers, and cut  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111920,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up factory cakes into individual portions. \nAs institutions of quantity over quality,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117120,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nothing had to be spectacular. \nIf a dish ran out or tasted bad,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120880,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there were tens of other options for \ncustomers to satisfy themselves with. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124320,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Compared to conventional restaurants where \nevery item on the menu must earn its price,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128080,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buffays just had to keep their pans full. \nThe skill floor was lower, the concept  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132240,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sold itself, a single family could run the \nentire kitchen, customers served themselves,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136880,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the whole thing ran on volume. \nWithin a decade, just about every  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140480,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Midwest town had a Chinese buffet.\nOn paper, the business made sense for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144720,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both independents and chains. \nA buffet could serve more people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147920,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "without needing more staff, and the labor \ncost savings, even with greater food waste,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152080,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would lead to higher profits. \nMass-market chains like Wendy’s,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "KFC, Burger King, and Sizzler all jumped on \nthe bandwagon and started bolting buffets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160720,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "onto the stores they already owned. \nUnder this lens, the dedicated operators  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like Fresh Choice, hometown, Sweet Tomatoes, and \nOld Country Buffet all looked like the next big  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170640,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "American restaurant empires. \nBut fast forward 20 years  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173760,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and almost nothing is left. \nEvery one of these former giants  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176960,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has gone bankrupt and their present-day owners \nhave publicly committed to keeping them buried. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181680,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The mass-market buffets still \nstanding today are the ones that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184720,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "never had to make money in the first place. \nResorts and cruises only offer buffets as a way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to drive higher prices for a package experience. \nStadiums, airlines, and credit card companies run  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195280,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buffets for their highest-paying customers \nand only in a handful of tiny lounges and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199920,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clubs that are never meant to scale.\nThe few lavish buffets that do exist  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203920,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around the world are generally \nfunded by hotels as loss leaders to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207680,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "generate demand for the rooms upstairs. \nEven in Vegas, the category is dying. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212400,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 2000, there were roughly \n35 buffets in the city. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215760,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today there are just 7 and it's rumored that \n3 more will close in the next 2 years. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The few still around run 90 minute time limits \nand charge close to a hundred dollars a person. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225360,
    "dDurationMs": 3276,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even Rio’s Carnival World Buffet, the first mover \nthat kicked off the local boom back in 1993,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228636,
    "dDurationMs": 2404,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has remained closed since the pandemic. \nThe only all-you-can-eat concepts that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are thriving today are niches like Chinese hot \npot, Korean barbeque, and Brazilian steakhouse,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236720,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where the menus are tight, rooms are small, check \nsizes are mid to high, and the appeal lies in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242240,
    "dDurationMs": 1807,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cuisine, rather than the format. \nEven the legacy Chinese buffet is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244047,
    "dDurationMs": 2033,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disappearing across the nation. \nIn short, the big-box value buffet  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246080,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is going extinct and the ones that are \nstill around or being built only survive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249920,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as amenities attached to a separate business. \nIn this episode, we analyze the entire industry,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255360,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from their public financials to their \nbankruptcy filings, to work out what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259360,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually happened to the American buffet and \nwhy the version of it that everybody loved is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263520,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the one version that could never survive.\nTo understand the buffet boom of the 80s,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267920,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we have to look at what came before. \nCafeterias were the predecessor to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272160,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "modern buffet, where Americans pushed trays \ndown an aisle, food sat warming on steam tables,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277280,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "servers portioned each dish onto plates, \nand every item had its own price. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281920,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Chains like Morrison’s and Luby's built \ntheir empires over decades on this simple  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formula of value and consistency but by the \nlate 70s, the format had become stale. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The single-file line was slow, the rooms felt \nsterile, and the rationing made these places feel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296000,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more like schools or hospitals. \nThe buffet chains of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299040,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "80s ripped up this status quo. \nThey broke the line into scatter bar stations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303040,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so diners could walk straight to whatever they \nwanted instead of waiting behind everyone else. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312320,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They let people serve themselves as much \nor as little of anything as they pleased. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316640,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And they simplified pricing \ninto one single number, so every  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319760,
    "dDurationMs": 2356,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "customer knew the cost before walking in. \nThe hairnets and ladles disappeared while  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322116,
    "dDurationMs": 2524,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "food choices grew from dozens to hundreds. \nWhile the legacy cafeterias and independents  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324640,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dumped food into pans, the upstarts \nlike Old Country, Sweet Tomatoes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329120,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Golden Corral, and Fresh Choice \nleaned into open kitchen theaters. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333520,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Workers by design pulled baked goods \nout of ovens, carved meat to order,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337600,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tossed salads in big bowls, and swapped out \ntrays regularly so food never looked old. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342480,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The equipment underneath was all the \nsame steam tables and warming pans  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345760,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that cafeterias had used for decades. \nMost of the food came frozen in bulk from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350400,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wholesale suppliers and the buffet’s only job \nwas to bring it up to serving temperature. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354960,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like dollar stores or pawn shops, \nbuffets do well when times are tough. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359040,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the average American household, \nthe 80s and 90s were just that tight. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363120,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This was the era of Reaganomics \nand globalization. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366000,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The economy grew under tax cuts, deregulation, \ntrade agreements, and the invention of the PC,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372000,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but this boom never reached the middle class. \nWages had been stagnant since the mid-70s. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377440,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Manufacturing employment peaked in \n79 and never came back, as factory  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381760,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "jobs got outsourced to Mexico or Asia and then \nreplaced by service jobs that paid far less. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387600,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Meanwhile, prices increased and \nminimum wage stayed frozen. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391200,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The same paycheck bought less every \nyear, for 20 years straight. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394720,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "American households made up this difference in \ntwo ways - first by turning the conventional  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398880,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stay-at-home wife into a second earner and \nthen second, by adopting credit cards. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403520,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By 1990, more than half of all women had jobs. \nAt the same time, household debt grew from 60% of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "income to over 90% by 2000, as banks \nflooded mailboxes with pre-approved  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415040,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "credit cards and encouraged overspending \nto make fortunes off interest and fees. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419440,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All of this would come home to roost less than \na decade later with the Great Recession. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423840,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in the interim of the 80s \nand 90s, the math was simple. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427440,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No parent would earn enough \nfor the family by themselves,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430240,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the gap between white collar and blue collar was \nwidening, and moms were no longer home to cook. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435600,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dinner needed to be fast, hot, filling, \nand cheap enough for the whole family. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440240,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The buffets filled this gap perfectly. \nFor the middle class and seniors on Social  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444400,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Security, it delivered unprecedented \nabundance and affordability. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448080,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The buffet was a fixed cost that could be planned \ninto any budget and if cheaper prices meant having  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452880,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to serve yourself, most were happy to do it. \nEating out anywhere else meant an open-ended  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457600,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bill that jumped with every added dish and tip. \nAmericans were already making this same tradeoff  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462480,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of less service for lower prices and \ngreater volume at Walmart, Costco,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466560,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Southwest Airlines. \nThe buffet was that same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469360,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bargain applied to lunch and dinner.\nThe biggest was Buffays incorporated,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473200,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who was the parent company behind \nOld Country and Hometown Buffet. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476880,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Their strategy was the simplest. \nThey believed that the format didn’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480160,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need refining, only multiplying. \nOld Country was the classic American  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484080,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buffet in its purest form - a big-box \nwith a price on a sign located in strip  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488640,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "centers across the Midwest and Northeast. \nHometown Buffet was its West Coast twin. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 10 years, the chain went from 1 location \nto 200 and then doubled again in 5. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498640,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At its peak, it opened more \nthan one location a week. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501600,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And when the two chains merged in \n1996, they became the biggest and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505520,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fastest-growing buffay operator in the world, \ngrossing nearly a billion dollars a year. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510160,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yet their rapid success \ncreated a fatal blind spot. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513280,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They had built a muscle for opening \nbuffets, but not for keeping customers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516960,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the two turned out to be \ncompletely different businesses. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520160,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Old Country and hometown had no \nreal branding, no signature dish,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523760,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no trademark recipe, or iconic product. \nThey served the same comfort food that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528240,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cafeterias had been serving for decades \nprior, competed exclusively on price,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532640,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and advertised quantity over quality. \nYet for millions of Americans this was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537200,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first all-you-can-eat buffet they \nhad ever seen and where food was laid  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541200,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out in islands - and being first was enough \nto carry both chains through the eighties.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545760,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The company bet everything on the idea \nthat scale alone would compound. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549680,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "More stores meant more purchasing \npower, more advertising leverage,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553120,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and more density, and all of it would naturally \nflow through to stronger per-store economics. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With 17% restaurant-level margins in the early \n90s, no one dared to question this playbook. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563520,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was the same playbook that Olive Garden and \nMcDonald's had used when they were young. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out of nowhere, sales started to drop. \nSame-store sales went negative in 1994 and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stayed in the red for 5 straight years. Sales \nflatlined for over a decade and fell behind  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578080,
    "dDurationMs": 768,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inflation, which meant that the real volume \nof the average Old Country and Hometown Buffet  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578848,
    "dDurationMs": 1792,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was shrinking the entire time. \nIt happened at Old Country Buffet,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580640,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it happened at HomeTown Buffet, \nwhich was still a separate company  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583920,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "run by a rival founder before the merger. \nNothing at the restaurants had changed. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588320,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The buffets were still the same, nearly \nforty percent of both chains had been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "opened or remodeled within the past two years, \nand the price increases had been marginal. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597280,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After the merger of the two giants, \nthey literally had no equal. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600640,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yet the buffets simply stopped drawing the crowd \nthey used to, and no one could figure out why.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605600,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was like Americans had suddenly \ndecided, starting in the mid-nineties,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that they had had enough and somehow the novelty \nof the format and the chain had worn off. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613920,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The company tried one fix after another but \nall of them were just one year band-aids.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618320,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 95, they slashed advertising to less \nthan 1% of sales to drive higher profits,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623440,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only to end up with the worst same-store \nsales in company history the year after. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627680,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Only when they tripled the advertising budget did \nsame-store sales turn positive again, which meant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632320,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the company had to keep marketing and giving \nup margin just to get customers in the door. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636960,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Labor costs rose every single \nyear for 6 years even as the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640240,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "company cut staffing to try and escape it. \nThe one cost that improved was food, and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644800,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "company only achieved it by riding the underlying \ncommodity prices for meat and produce. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649520,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Expansion never outran the \nper-store decline. Instead,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652880,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it only produced more underperforming stores. \nBut no matter what it cut, the chain never  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657600,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regained its double digit margins and any profits \nin the decade after were simply borrowed from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662560,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "elsewhere in the business, rather than earned. \nWith falling traffic, the last thing they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667280,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dared was to raise the price.\nWith negative same-store sales  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for 5 straight years, the company’s valuation \ncollapsed as a growth stock without the growth. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By 2000, the company was trying everything \nexcept buffays in search of a new growth engine:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680640,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "investing in steakhouses, roadhouse \ngrills, even pizza arcades. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684800,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "None of them caught on, and the \nbusiness devolved into a cash machine. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688640,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With $90 million a year in cash flow \nand minimal debt, Buffays Inc became a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693120,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perfect target for a private equity takeover. \nOn paper, a PE owner could ruthlessly trim fat,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698800,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "maximize yield, and use the existing cash flow \nto cover the interest on the debt needed to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703440,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "take over the company in the first place. \nAnd after all the value had been extracted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707440,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the remaining customers, the company could \nbe flipped back onto the public markets with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "better-looking numbers for a bigger payout. \nA PE firm out in New York City followed this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716960,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exact playbook and borrowed over half a billion \ndollars to take Buffays Inc private in 2000. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722480,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yet traffic never improved once \nin their 6 years of ownership. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The PE firm immediately raised prices, gutted \nstaffing and service, and tried to compensate for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these changes through promos— all of which only \nfurther alienated customers and eroded profits.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737360,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By 2005, it had loaded up the once-healthy company \nwith debt, harvested $90M in dividends for itself  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743840,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "along the way, and left a bloated carcass of \na business that was paying more in interest to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748080,
    "dDurationMs": 2341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lenders than it was earning from restaurants. \nThe buffays were still generating positive margin  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750421,
    "dDurationMs": 91,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the price increases, but all that profit \nwas vacuumed up to service the debt that had  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750512,
    "dDurationMs": 2208,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "made the takeover possible in the first place. \nTo make matters worse, the company was slipping  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752720,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "further and further away from a billion dollar in \nsales, which made an IPO less likely every year. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758720,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The only remaining short-term option was to \nsimply buy the numbers needed for an exit. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763040,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Ryan’s was just a larger \ndose of the same disease. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765840,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Southern buffet chain had boomed in the 80s \nand was shrinking even faster than Old Country  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Hometown at the time of its purchase. \nThe acquisition was financed by selling the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775120,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "land underneath most of Ryan's restaurants, \nwhich turned the one asset that didn't depend  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779200,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on traffic into a new annual rent bill. \nWith interest and rent stacked on top of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783760,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each other and all three chains still \nlosing customers, the restaurants could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787440,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no longer cover what the company owed, and \nthe 2008 recession finished the math. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792320,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bankruptcy only prolonged the \ndeath for another decade. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795120,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It cut the debt and eliminated the worst \nstores but did nothing to fix the number  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798880,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that really mattered, which was demand. \nThe company spent the next 13 years in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803280,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and out of bankruptcy before finally \nflatlining in 2021 during the pandemic.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808240,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fresh Choice tried to solve the economics from the \nother end by pushing cheap salads and carbs over  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the expensive proteins that had driven up food \ncosts at Old Country, Hometown, and Ryan’s. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818480,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like the rest of the industry, it had \nskyrocketed to popularity in the late 80s as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822800,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a healthy buffet riding on the low-fat and \nlow-sodium craze sweeping the nation. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827280,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The chain advertised itself as the most affordable \nway for Americans to hit the recommended nine  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "daily servings of fruits and vegetables. \nEvery store was built around a massive salad  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837040,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bar and a build-your-own section with \nindividual ingredients and dressings. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841200,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After the salads, there was soup, hot pasta, \nbaked potatoes, muffins, fruit, and desserts. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847520,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Produce was delivered 4 times a week \nand everything was made from scratch. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851600,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The chain concentrated itself \nin Silicon Valley, where its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854800,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "healthy positioning landed the hardest. \nYet with virtually no meat and no entree,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fresh Choice was pressured to compete on value. \nThey charged customers in Northern California  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864480,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same prices as Old Country \nand Hometown Buffet in Ohio. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868240,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And just like its biggest rival, the chain \nonly lost money and customers as it grew.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872800,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fresh Choice went public in the early 90s and \nused the money to become a national chain under  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877200,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the pursuit of economies of scale. \nIt launched stores in Seattle, Dallas,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and DC, and clustered the openings so that costs \ncould be spread across all locations at once. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886320,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But every market outside California turned out to \nbe far less profitable as costs only went up the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "further the company moved away from its California \nsupply chain and the clustered stores cannibalized  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896480,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each other's sales instead of growing them. \nWithin two years, the company was shuttering  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900720,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new stores by the dozen and cutting costs \neverywhere it could to restore the profits  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904800,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lost to expansion. \nBy 1996, 90% of all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908640,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fresh Choices were back in California.\nWithout any remaining nationwide ambitions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913200,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the company could only turn inward to grow. \nIt moved to one flat price  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917040,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and eliminated discounts. \nIt added chicken to salads and rolled  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out richer soups to justify price increases. \nIt tried a smaller, more efficient, and cheaper  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925760,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "layout for new stores while remodeling old ones. \nBut none of it helped. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929600,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Customers continually refused to pay more \nwithout a real entree and every investment  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934240,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meant to justify a price increase \nonly accelerated customer churn. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938400,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yet the company couldn’t give in because \nthe absence of meat was its profit. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942160,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Despite charging the same prices as its Midwest \nrivals and operating in a far more expensive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "market, their food costs were consistently \n10% lower than Hometown and Old Country. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952000,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This gap was the entire margin. \nBut even with the food cost advantage,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956000,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fresh Choice found itself learning the same \nhard lessons as Buffays Incorporated. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960480,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The format had a ceiling far lower \nthan imagined and the chains had  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hit this maturity cap on a per-unit and \nnetwork basis far faster than expected. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Customers were proving to be absurdly resistant to \nany price increase and coupons remained the only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973760,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proven way to get people through the door.\nBy the late 90s, the declining traffic and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978400,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "non-existent pricing power had sunk Fresh Choice \ninto annual losses and penny stock territory. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983680,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The company brought in new leaders who came \nin preaching to do more with less and through  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987520,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ruthless cost control, they briefly squeezed \nprofit out of a shrinking customer base. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992000,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whenever an ingredient got expensive, workers \npulled it off the line and replaced it with a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996480,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cheaper one until prices settled, so every item \ngot managed by cost rather than experience. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1002400,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Recipes that took too much \nlabor were simplified. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005120,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the absence of meat, Fresh Choice leaned \nharder into cheap starches like pizza,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009360,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "potatoes, pasta, and cobbler. \nChicken and tuna got pulled off  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012720,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the line and sold as paid add-ons. \nUnder these policies, food and labor  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "costs hit record lows, check sizes increased, \ncoupons boosted traffic, and the company was able  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022400,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to squeeze out one final profitable year. \nYet when the coupons were discontinued the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1027120,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "following year, sales crashed immediately, \nturnover spiked, and stores closed. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031920,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To offset decline, the company made \none last attempt at cheap growth. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036240,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fresh Choice Express were tiny satellite \nlocations stocked with food from the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040160,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nearest buffet and repackaged as grab-and-go \nmeals for office workers and hotel guests. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1045280,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The concept failed quickly as \nit solved the wrong thing. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048160,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The problem was not that new Fresh \nChoices were too expensive to open,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1051920,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but instead fewer and fewer customers were \nshowing up at the ones that were already built.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1056720,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The free-fall continued into the 2000s. \nFood costs stayed at record lows,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "yet labor costs kept growing despite staff \ncuts, driven by California's rising minimum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1066240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wage, healthcare costs, and workers' comp. \nBy the time the Atkins diet and low-carb trend  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1071680,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reached California, the buffet looked obsolete by \nmodern standards and the only adaptation that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1076800,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "company could afford was sugar-free Jello. \nLike Hometown and Old Country before,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1081360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nothing worked - and in 2004, Fresh Choice became \nthe first of the big 3 to file for bankruptcy. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087360,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The company would persist on life support \nfor another 8 years recycling the same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1091360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tactics - adding new items to generate traffic, \nremoving them in the next to claw back costs,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1096480,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "offering discounts to buy sales, pulling \nthem in the next to restore margin,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1100080,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and shuttering the worst performers - all because \nthe business couldn’t hold its customer base. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1104880,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By 2012, Fresh Choice was down to just \n20 locations and by the end of the year,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1109680,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the whole company had shut down. \nIts final owner later reopened two  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1113680,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stores under a new name and gave up the \nbuffet entirely, opting to sell salad and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1118240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sandwiches by the pound and calling the format \nunsustainable with no place in the modern age.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1264320,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sweet Tomatoes, which was also known as \nSoup Plantation, was the most efficient  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1268240,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and disciplined operator within the \nindustry, and it still fell apart. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1272160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It made itself a destination from the get-go, \nbuilding their buffets as freestanding restaurants  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1277120,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with private parking on land it owned, instead \nof leasing in strip malls like its main rivals. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1282160,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every Sweet Tomatoes had a 55-foot salad bar, \n8 soups made from scratch, a baked potato bar,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1288080,
    "dDurationMs": 4901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a dessert bar, a pasta station, and a bakery \nthat churned out muffins and focaccia. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1292981,
    "dDurationMs": 100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Behind the salad there was endless clam chowder, \nchili, alfredo, muffins, and pizza, so nobody left  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1293081,
    "dDurationMs": 1799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hungry just because there was no meat.\nThe chain had pushed signature dishes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1294880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "since the 80s, like Tuna Tarragon and Joan's \nBroccoli Madness. These branded items injected  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1300400,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "invaluable credibility that Souplantation \nwasn't another bottom-of-the-barrel buffet  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1304880,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "competing on abundance alone. \nThe company was also far more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1308240,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strategic about where it operated. \nFresh Choice had plowed money into leasing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1312400,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the most expensive retail spots available \nunder the belief that the highest educated,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1317040,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "affluent customers would resonate \nbest with the healthy branding. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1320880,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In contrast, Sweet Tomatoes picked \nlocations that could capture both  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1324400,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lunch and dinner near business districts and \nshopping centers rather than inside them. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1329120,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It bought the land wherever it could or \nsecured extra-long 30 year leases, all of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1333600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which enabled the company to outlast its rivals. \nIt applied clustering to both the prep and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1338800,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stores, building 19 central kitchens \nto do the scratch cooking in bulk and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1342800,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deliver the finished food to each buffet. \nThis way, food cost and quality control got  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1347600,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "managed centrally, instead of being left in the \nhands of the hourly worker running each station. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1352480,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And when it came time to expand, the \ncompany kept new locations close to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1356080,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its central kitchens to keep them running at \ncapacity instead of chasing faraway markets.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1361360,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But logic and discipline only \ndelayed the inevitable. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1364480,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sweet Tomatoes used its funds to expand \nacross the Sunbelt, choosing the states  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1368480,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the greatest retiree populations. \nThrough the 90s, the company enjoyed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1372560,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the best same-store sales and \nmargins in the entire industry. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1375920,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there were signs that even the company \ndidn’t believe the good times would last. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1380080,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At its peak in 1999, it tried pre-packaging \nthe same soups, salads, and breads from the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1385520,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buffet for takeout but the concept never \ntook off just like Fresh Choice Express.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1389920,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But after a decade of non-stop growth, the \ncustomer base suddenly petered out even as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1394240,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the chain opened new locations at record pace. \nBy 2001, traffic had plummeted and business fell  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1400400,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with it even though prices stayed the same. \nWith guests vanishing, costs ballooned, and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1405200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "company’s double digit margins got cut in half. \nAll store openings were cancelled and the chain  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1410080,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "desperately sold off the land \nit owned to restore margin. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1413280,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yet demand never recovered, traffic kept \nfalling, and the business offset the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1417200,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lost sales through price hikes. \nHaving seen its rivals fall apart  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1420640,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the exact same way, Sweet Tomato's \nowners quickly sold to private equity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1424720,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to avoid going down with the sinking ship. \nIts new PE owners sold off the remaining land  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1429600,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for themselves and left behind a carcass \nstripped of its most valuable assets. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1434000,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The chain would go on to change hands \nthree times over the next fifteen years. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1438240,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When the pandemic hit, the company had \nno cash flow to pay the millions in rent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1442240,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "owed and the entire business died within weeks. \nHad Sweet Tomatoes still owned its land,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1447120,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it could have hibernated through the pandemic. \nBut even then, it would have just limped into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1450880,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the present-day hyperinflation - kept \nalive only by the ground it stood on.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1455280,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s no coincidence that these \nthree giants all met the same fate.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1458800,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fresh Choice had the cheapest food \nand died because its customers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1462160,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wouldn’t pay more than $7 for dinner. \nSweet Tomatoes ran the most efficient  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1466400,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "operation and lasted only as long as it did \nby owning the land beneath its restaurants. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1471040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Buffays Inc had the biggest footprint, the \ncheapest labor, the greatest economies of scale,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1476080,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and still couldn't raise prices without \nlosing the customers it needed to survive. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1480400,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ultimately, it was the fast food and casual \nchains who had jumped on the bandwagon back  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1484720,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the 80s that ended up reaching this \nconclusion first. By the late 90s,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1489280,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wendy's, Burger King, and KFC had all shut down \ntheir salad bars and all-you-can-eat deals,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1495040,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because none of it made money. \nIn contrast, the buffet chains had  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1498560,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nothing else to sell and had no choice \nbut to ride the format into the ground.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1502320,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like the dinosaurs, the buffet was killed \nby the ecosystem changing around it. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1506480,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first threat came from \ninside the grocery store. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1508960,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The freezer aisle had grown up \nalongside the buffet in the 80s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1512080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and sold the time-starved American middle class \nthe same promise of dinner without cooking. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1517040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lean Cuisine launched in 1981 and grossed over \na hundred million dollars in its first year. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1522320,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The microwave quickly turned into an \nessential fixture of the modern American  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1526080,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kitchen and by the early 90s, the freezer \naisle was saturated with premium brands. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1530880,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For a while, it looked like these frozen \nmeals would disrupt the buffet entirely,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1534800,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the reality is that it never did. \nThe frozen dinner was built on individual portions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1539360,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while the buffet was built for families and so \nthe two never competed directly with each other. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1544080,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the same time, once nutrition labels \nbecame mandatory in the mid 90s, Americans  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1548800,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were shocked to find out the extreme levels of \nsodium, fats, and additives that had made the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1553840,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "frozen meals so palatable in the first place. \nBy the late ’90s, Americans were losing interest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1559120,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in both frozen dinners and buffets, as traffic and \nsales declined across both categories. But they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1564880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weren’t going back to cooking at home—so something \nelse had clearly stepped in to meet that demand.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1570080,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead, dinner had simply moved 20 feet over—to \nthe deli counter in the same supermarket.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1575360,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The economic stagnation of the 80s and \n90s had been a boom for not just buffays,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1579680,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also new ready-to-eat takeout concepts like \nBoston Market, Kenny Rogers, and El Pollo Loco. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1585840,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These restaurants were designed for the \nmiddle class and specialized in serving hot,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1589920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ready-to-eat meals for the entire family. \nFor ten dollars or less, Americans got rotisserie  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1595280,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chicken, mashed potatoes, cornbread, and \nsides - all less processed than frozen,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1600480,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "less greasy than fast food, and cheaper, \nfaster, and more consistent than a restaurant. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1605680,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The rapid rise of all three categories throughout \nthe ’80s and ’90s—buffets, premium frozen meals,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1611600,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and now take-home dinner chains—made \nAmerica’s grocers increasingly nervous.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1616080,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a world that was now too busy \nto cook, supermarkets appeared  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1619200,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be increasingly irrelevant selling \njust dry goods and raw ingredients. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1623360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Grocers began to realize that what customers \nreally wanted was a cheap, hot, non-frozen dinner  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1628480,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they didn’t have to cook and would fit easily into \ntheir schedule. If supermarkets wanted to win back  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1633280,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that basket size and market share, they would have \nto start selling that convenience themselves.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1637840,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the mid-’90s, supermarkets across America \nwere investing heavily in deli departments,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1642560,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "adding hot prepared foods and \nrotisserie chickens of their own.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1646000,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fast forward to present day, and Costco’s \nrotisserie chicken has become a household staple,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1651040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whole Foods has normalized picking up dinner from \na hot bar, and nearly every major supermarket now  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1656240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has a full prepared-foods section stocked \nwith chicken, sides, sandwiches, sushi,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1661680,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and other ready-to-eat meals.\nThe supermarkets could make  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1665040,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these investments because they never \nneeded the deli to carry the store. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1668560,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They priced by the pound and per item, since they \ndidn't need unlimited portions to drive traffic. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1673600,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Americans were already coming in \nevery week for their milk and eggs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1676560,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the deli was a cross-sell with \nzero customer acquisition cost. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1680480,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It shared labor and overhead with the rest of \nthe store, so a deli worker could be pulled  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1684880,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to staff another department whenever \nthe counter was slow and vice versa. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1688720,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A buffet, by contrast, had to compel customers \nto make the trip while paying rent on the entire  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1693680,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dining room at every hour of the day. \nWhatever a buffet couldn't sell went in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1697760,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the dumpster, whereas the supermarket \nhad a downstream use for everything. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1701760,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unsold rotisserie chicken became chicken \nsalad, unsold ingredients turned into sides,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1706880,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and anything close to expiring got \nmarked down at the end of the day. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1710880,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even today, supermarkets discount their \nprepared foods frequently and aggressively,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1715520,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "often positioning them as loss leaders because \nthey know they can make that margin back on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1720320,
    "dDurationMs": 2178,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "everything else that ends up in the basket.\nAll this enabled supermarkets to match buffays  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1722498,
    "dDurationMs": 92,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on value, undercut the surging Boston Market \non price, and outperform both on convenience. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1722590,
    "dDurationMs": 87,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Supermarkets already kept far longer hours \nthan most restaurants, so serving customers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1722677,
    "dDurationMs": 82,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "early in the morning, late at night, or \nanytime in between required little change  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1722759,
    "dDurationMs": 2521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from how they had operated for decades. \nThus, it was supermarkets—and three decades  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1725280,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of continuous investment in delis, hot \nbars, and prepared foods—that ultimately  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1729760,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hollowed out the bottom of the market.\nFrom this lens, the only place left for a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1733840,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buffet to survive is upmarket. \nAt Brazilian steakhouses,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1737360,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the servers control the pace and portion, \nwhich limits food cost and reduces waste. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1742080,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And at Korean barbecue and hot pot, you cook \nthe food yourself, which slows consumption,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1747120,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stretches out the meal, and gives your stomach \ntime to digest - all of which naturally limits  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1752160,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how much you order in a single sitting and by \nextension, controls the restaurant's food cost. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1757280,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The legacy chains had none of these mechanisms. In \norder to be something for everyone at the lowest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1762160,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "price point possible, they had no choice \nbut to water down everything at scale.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1766480,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While supermarkets took over the bottom of \nthe market, fast casual killed the top. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1770960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No industry leader could have imagined back in the \n80s that an entirely new category would emerge,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1776320,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "offering the same customization, \nconsistency, abundance, and pricing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1780720,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but with better ingredients, greater \ntransparency, and far more profit. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1785040,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The girl who went to Fresh Choice in the \n90s for salad with her family upgraded  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1789120,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to Sweetgreen in the 2010s. \nThe family that piled plates at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1792800,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hometown or Sweet Tomatoes transitioned \nto buying burrito bowls at Chipotle. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1797280,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fast casual was built from the ground up for a \nnew generation that prized quality over quantity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1802080,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and needed takeout far more than dine-in. \nMeanwhile, the buffet was stuck with stores,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1806480,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stations, and service built \nexclusively for eating on the spot. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1809600,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It made its money when you got full and \nthere was no world where they could box  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1813280,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up leftovers or deliver to your door. \nAt the same time, the nightly family  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1817200,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dinner of the 70s and 80s has become a relic. \nWith gig work, longer commutes, longer shifts,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1822560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and shorter job tenures, households today eat \nindividually at different times and more people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1827680,
    "dDurationMs": 2649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eat alone with a phone than together at a table. \nThe supermarket deli, delivery apps, and fast  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1830329,
    "dDurationMs": 71,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "casual chains are all infrastructure \nbuilt for this modern lifestyle. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1830400,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From this lens, fast casual was the iterative \nsuccessor to the buffet the same way the buffet  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1835440,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was the successor to the cafeteria.\nBut despite the external disruption,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1839440,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the buffet business was still \nfundamentally cursed from the inside. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1843200,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Its unit economics were built on \na labor structure that could only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1846400,
    "dDurationMs": 2933,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "work in the stagnation of the 80s and 90s. \nThe restaurant business is a simple formula  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1849333,
    "dDurationMs": 1307,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of food, labor, and land. \nThe entire investor pitch  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1850640,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "behind the buffet boom was low labor costs. \nBut in practice, Fresh Choice, Sweet Tomatoes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1856080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Old Country all needed as much labor, if \nnot more, than a conventional restaurant. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1861040,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Someone has to heat the food, fill the trays, \nbuss the tables, and wash all the plates. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1865840,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These were all grueling, low-skill, minimum \nwage jobs, which was fine in the 70s, 80s,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1870960,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and 90s when the federal minimum \nwage sat frozen at 3 dollars and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1874800,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the labor pool was deep with displaced \nfactory workers and non-graduates. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1879200,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But as minimum wage climbed \n— the entire equation broke.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1882560,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There was no way to offset the increasing \nlabor cost except by further reducing staff,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1887040,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which only worsened product and service. \nThey couldn’t ask for tips either as the chains  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1891520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had spent generations marketing no tip - and \nthe labor problem was deeper than just wages. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1896480,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The category had no ladder. \nThe buffet business is so unusual  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1900000,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that work experience doesn’t \nreally transfer anywhere else. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1903040,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's not fast food, fast casual, or a \nconventional full-service restaurant.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1907520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Batch cooking only transfers to institutional \nkitchens like hospitals, cafeterias, prisons,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1912640,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or catering, where the wages \nand hours are generally worse. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1916160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thus, even if the big buffet chains had paid \nwell, it wouldn’t have stopped the turnover. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1921120,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The work would have remained \nunglamorous, low-status,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1923840,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and marginalized within the industry. \nThe buffet could only keep its workers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1927920,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with cash and yet cash was the \none thing they couldn't afford. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1931280,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And so they all kept pay as low as possible, let \npeople quit, and hired whoever came next, rather  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1936880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than train, promote, and reward their talent.\nFood is the other half of the equation and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1942080,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the buffet controlled little of it. \nRestaurants live or die based on their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1945760,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "food costs - and food costs are a \nfunction of price and portion. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1949200,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A buffet has no portions and one single \nprice, which means that customers are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1953680,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the ones that dictate food cost. \nEven during their golden age, the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1957440,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buffet chains incurred substantially higher food \ncosts than any other category of restaurant. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1962560,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The second driver behind these \nhigh food costs was waste. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1965600,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A buffet must sell abundance and \nthat generosity must be visible. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1969280,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Half-empty trays look bad and so trays \nmust generally stay full until closing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1973920,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even if that means cooking food no one will eat \nand throwing it away at the end of the night. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1978480,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The entire model runs on the law of averages, \nwhere the business nets a positive margin  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1982880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on light eaters like seniors, women, and \nchildren and uses that profit to subsidize  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1988080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the losses from teenage boys and binge eaters. \nThis hedge only works as long as the customer base  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1993440,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stays broad and traffic remains consistent. \nWhen the clientele shrinks down to the heavy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1997920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eaters, the entire business falls apart. \nThe reality is that rising food and labor costs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2003280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have squeezed the entire industry since the 80s, \nbut every other category has had levers to pull. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2008640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we look at McDonald’s today, the company openly \npushes food and labor costs onto franchisees who  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2013920,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in turn pass on these downstream effects \nonto customers - pricing Big Macs for $10,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2019040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shutting dine-in rooms, forcing customers to \nself-service kiosks, and rolling out sloppier and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2024400,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smaller items so workers can do more with less. \nWendy’s has fared far worse under the same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2029680,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pressures - they’ve slashed product \ndown to the bone and hiked prices and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2033120,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in turn have lost customers faster \nthan the higher checks can cover. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2036800,
    "dDurationMs": 90,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cheesecake Factory has coped by keeping \nits footprint small, opening only where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2036890,
    "dDurationMs": 107,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the numbers work, and raising prices to maintain \nthe labor-intensive scratch cooking that made  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2036997,
    "dDurationMs": 1883,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the chain famous in the first place. \nMeanwhile, Shake Shack has adapted to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2038880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "labor costs by cutting store staff, engineering \nlocations to run with as few workers as possible,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2044320,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and aggressively diluting the product \nand menu to match the fewer personnel. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2048560,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ultimately, every restaurant has 4 levers to \nget to profit - raise price, shrink portion,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2053760,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cut labor, or shrink the space. \nThe buffet chains had none of these.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2057760,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They couldn’t raise prices, they couldn’t \ncut labor, they couldn’t shrink portions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2061680,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and they couldn’t shrink the room.\nSince the 90s, the entire restaurant industry  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2065520,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has been focused on doing more in less space. \nBut buffays can never really be efficient. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2070320,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They need the square footage to hold the variety \nthat make them desirable in the first place and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2074720,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when they start big, they have to stay big. \nThis worked in the 80s when land was cheap,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2079200,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "malls were sprouting everywhere, and landlords \ncared more about traffic than rent. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2083200,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But as malls fell out of favor, retail migrated, \nand e-commerce hollowed out the strip centers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2088400,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these buffet chains found themselves locked \ninto these 10,000 square foot boxes in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2093120,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "locations that no other tenant wanted. \nThis is why all the chains filed Chapter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2097520,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "11 repeatedly as bankruptcy was the only \nway to legally get out of hundreds of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2101680,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leases that nobody else was willing to take.\nGolden Corral today is the last man standing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2106560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and these days, it wields far greater pricing \npower than it did decades ago, but only because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2111120,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there’s no one left to compete with. \nWhile the upstarts of the 80s went public  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2115040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and borrowed billions to supercharge growth, \nGolden Corral survived by staying private. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2120320,
    "dDurationMs": 4289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It franchised almost everything, never sold \nits land, or borrowed back-breaking debt. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2124609,
    "dDurationMs": 80,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Golden Corral collects $50,000 from every \nnew franchisee, sells the development  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2124689,
    "dDurationMs": 73,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "services and kitchen equipment at a \nmarkup through its own subsidiaries,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2124762,
    "dDurationMs": 2598,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and takes 4% royalty on gross sales. \nThe company today owns just 3 restaurants  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2127360,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and every exposure that killed Sweet \nTomatoes, Fresh Choice, and Hometown  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2131600,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Buffet falls entirely on local operators. \nUnder this lens, Golden Corral just sells the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2137120,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "idea and template of a buffet and rents \nit out to anyone willing to buy it.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2141280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The average Golden Corral grossed $3.6 million \ndollars a year before the pandemic - which is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2146400,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "40% more than the average Hometown \nor Old Country before bankruptcy. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2150960,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the same time, the company has \nintentionally run the highest food  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2154000,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cost in the category for decades while every other \nchain historically starved it to protect margin. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2159600,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Golden Corral’s menu is full of branded products \nlike awesome pot roast, Bourbon Street chicken,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2165120,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fresh-baked rolls every 15 minutes, and grills \nwith steak cooked to order for dinner. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2169760,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These experiences all justified higher prices, \nmaintained the appeal of dine-in, and enabled rate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2174960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "limiting on the most expensive item on the menu. \nUltimately, franchisees only go as far as their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2180320,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "franchisor - and Golden Corral \nrules with an iron fist. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2184000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Franchisees are required to keep remodeling their \nstores so nothing ever looks or feels dated. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2189280,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The chain has also greatly benefited by being in \nthe South, where the cheaper land, cheaper labor,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2194800,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and thin competition has allowed the brand to \nbecome a generational institution for families,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2200160,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "churchgoers, and retirees in towns \nwhere Golden Corral remains the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2204560,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "biggest sit-down restaurant within 20 miles. \nIn contrast, Fresh Choice, Hometown, and Sweet  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2209360,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tomatoes never got that kind of permanence. \nTheir primary customers on the coasts were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2213680,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more affluent but far busier and transient - \nalways moving, always chasing the next thing. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2219040,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A buffet in a strip mall in Silicon Valley was \nnever going to become a Sunday tradition the way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2224000,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one could be in a small town in Georgia.\nYet Golden Corral, like any franchisor,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2228560,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a predator at heart. \nWhen a franchisee fails, the company simply  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2232400,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "offers the abandoned store to the next one. \nCOVID was a perfect example  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2236480,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this model in action. \nThe chain lost a quarter of its restaurants  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2239920,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in 2 years and yet the company never needed to \nshow up in court because those bankruptcies and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2244800,
    "dDurationMs": 3031,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "liabilities fell entirely on the franchisees. \nWhen Golden Corral’s largest franchisee went  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2247831,
    "dDurationMs": 98,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bankrupt, the company only lost its royalties.\nWithin the year, it was recruiting new franchisees  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2247929,
    "dDurationMs": 1511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to take over the stores that its former business \npartner had given up. In short, franchising never  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2249440,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cured the buffet’s fragile unit economics. \nIt simply insulates the company by pushing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2254240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the risk onto dozens of independent operators \nso that the debt, leases, risk, and traffic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2259200,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem never directly impact the brand. \nYet Golden Corral today is still shrinking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2264000,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even as it’s inherited the customers of \nevery buffet that’s closed around it. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2268000,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The company has shown no interest in rebuilding \nback to its pre-pandemic scale and refuses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2272640,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to count the reopened closures as growth. \nThe great American buffet of the 80s and 90s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2277520,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was a format for a specific era in history defined \nby cheap commodity food, cheap unskilled labor,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2283360,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cheap suburban land, wage stagnation, \nand a budget-minded middle class looking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2287920,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for a taste of affordable prosperity. \nYet all four inputs got more expensive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2292320,
    "dDurationMs": 1705,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the customer stopped wanting it. \nEven in Las Vegas, the gourmet buffets are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2294025,
    "dDurationMs": 97,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shrinking and the handful of legacy independents, \nniche chains, and upmarket boutiques today now  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2294122,
    "dDurationMs": 1558,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "effectively own the entire category. \nThis is one case where history is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2295680,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "guaranteed not to repeat. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2297280,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nobody is launching a new buffet \nchain, and nobody ever will. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2300560,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The sign by the road that promised all you could \neat for ten dollars or less isn't coming back. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2304960,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That deal is gone and the family that needed \nit found dinner somewhere else a long time ago."
    } ]
  } ]
}
